WEBVTT

00:00:06.295 --> 00:00:08.386
A sundial turns space into time.

00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:10.814
Its gnomon casts the shadow.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.921
Sunlight and geometry decide
where the shadow falls,

00:00:13.961 --> 00:00:15.427
and when it reaches each mark.

00:00:15.973 --> 00:00:19.530
Gnomon4D brings this idea to
embodied world-action models.

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.200
Learn where, and when, to focus.

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:28.256
Give a robot an instruction.

00:00:28.797 --> 00:00:30.882
A world-action model imagines the future,

00:00:30.922 --> 00:00:32.871
then generates the actions
that make it real,

00:00:32.911 --> 00:00:35.524
inheriting the priors of
large video generators.

00:00:38.262 --> 00:00:40.364
Existing models leave two gaps.

00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:43.480
First:

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.145
what should that future encode?

00:00:45.686 --> 00:00:48.981
RGB mixes motion with texture,
light, and background.

00:00:49.538 --> 00:00:52.220
Depth adds geometry, but
one frame at a time.

00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:55.742
It shows where surfaces are,
not which point went where.

00:00:57.895 --> 00:00:58.833
A second gap:

00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:00.202
where does the action look?

00:01:00.732 --> 00:01:04.243
We probed released world-action
models on fifty RoboTwin windows.

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:06.709
Their attention spreads over the arm.

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:10.391
The rest of the arm draws up to
two point three times uniform.

00:01:10.948 --> 00:01:13.578
The interaction region, the
object and the fingers around it,

00:01:13.618 --> 00:01:16.743
matters most for control, yet
gets at most one point five.

00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:22.395
Control hinges on two questions.

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.959
Where does the robot meet the object?

00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:27.720
And when does contact begin, or end?

00:01:28.266 --> 00:01:30.164
Gnomon4D answers both.

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:33.763
Three contributions.

00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:34.684
One:

00:01:34.724 --> 00:01:37.872
GeoDelta, an explicit 4D
motion representation.

00:01:38.429 --> 00:01:38.843
Two:

00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:42.611
InSAttn and TransAgg, which tell
the action where and when to focus.

00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:43.582
Three:

00:01:43.622 --> 00:01:47.059
one motion space for humans and robots,
so we pretrain on more than five

00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:48.815
thousand hours of human video.

00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:53.809
Gnomon4D has three experts.

00:01:54.339 --> 00:01:56.844
From the current frame, its
depth and foreground mask,

00:01:56.884 --> 00:01:59.537
and the instruction, it jointly
generates future video,

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:02.986
a 4D motion field called GeoDelta,
and the robot's actions.

00:02:05.162 --> 00:02:06.492
Contribution one.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:08.214
GeoDelta.

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:13.687
GeoDelta follows every pixel
on the arm and the object.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:15.796
We lift it to 3D.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.523
We find the same point four steps later.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.364
We project both into the source camera.

00:02:21.921 --> 00:02:23.222
The difference is its motion:

00:02:23.262 --> 00:02:25.694
delta u, delta v, and delta d.

00:02:27.962 --> 00:02:30.701
Optical flow misses motion
along the line of sight.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.742
Delta d captures it.

00:02:33.298 --> 00:02:35.128
Depth stays inside one frame.

00:02:35.685 --> 00:02:38.833
Every GeoDelta value links two
moments of the same point.

00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:43.145
A signed mu-law turns it into color, so
the frozen video autoencoder can encode

00:02:43.185 --> 00:02:45.901
it, aligned pixel for pixel
with the future video.

00:02:49.162 --> 00:02:52.253
All three experts denoise
together, through joint attention.

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:56.169
GeoDelta and actions run on a
clock twice as fast as video.

00:02:56.714 --> 00:02:58.817
Halfway through, the actions are ready.

00:02:59.373 --> 00:03:01.964
The robot moves while the
video keeps refining.

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:05.704
Contribution two.

00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:08.051
InSAttn and TransAgg.

00:03:08.608 --> 00:03:10.517
Where, and when, to focus.

00:03:12.862 --> 00:03:14.958
Joint attention sees every future token,

00:03:14.998 --> 00:03:16.464
but not which ones matter.

00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:18.517
So we compare motions.

00:03:19.051 --> 00:03:21.710
As the gripper approaches,
the block stays still.

00:03:22.255 --> 00:03:25.528
Their motions differ, so the
relative-motion field c is high.

00:03:26.073 --> 00:03:29.210
Once grasped, they move
together, and c drops to zero.

00:03:29.755 --> 00:03:33.562
Interaction-Salient Attention,
InSAttn, reads where c is high.

00:03:35.995 --> 00:03:37.211
Now, time.

00:03:37.764 --> 00:03:39.423
A grasp switches c off.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.695
A release switches it back on.

00:03:42.241 --> 00:03:44.474
Transition-Anchored Aggregation, TransAgg,

00:03:44.514 --> 00:03:47.780
weights each frame by this change, so
the action reads the moments that decide

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:48.593
the task.

00:03:51.029 --> 00:03:51.784
The result:

00:03:51.824 --> 00:03:52.961
attention flips.

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:56.740
The interaction region rises to
two point four times uniform.

00:03:57.297 --> 00:03:59.706
The rest of the arm falls below uniform.

00:04:02.462 --> 00:04:03.883
Contribution three.

00:04:04.423 --> 00:04:07.037
One motion space, for humans and robots.

00:04:09.262 --> 00:04:13.171
GeoDelta describes how surfaces
move, not how a body is controlled.

00:04:13.723 --> 00:04:16.740
So a human hand and a robot
gripper share one motion space,

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:17.973
with no retargeting.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.945
We pretrain on more than five thousand
hours of action-free human video,

00:04:21.985 --> 00:04:24.490
mid-train on more than thirty
hours of real-robot data,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.269
then post-train on each benchmark.

00:04:29.029 --> 00:04:30.597
Now, the results.

00:04:38.429 --> 00:04:41.497
Gnomon4D leads all three
simulation benchmarks.

00:04:42.038 --> 00:04:43.577
On RoboTwin 2.0:

00:04:43.617 --> 00:04:45.714
ninety-four point three
percent average success,

00:04:45.754 --> 00:04:48.992
over fifty bimanual tasks, in
clean and randomized scenes.

00:04:51.429 --> 00:04:55.343
On VLABench, its lead over OpenWAM-Alpha
grows from zero point six points in

00:04:55.383 --> 00:04:58.292
distribution to seven point
four under unseen textures.

00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:00.947
Motion does not change with appearance.

00:05:03.395 --> 00:05:07.242
On RoboDojo, it doubles the success rate
of the strongest world-action model,

00:05:07.282 --> 00:05:10.202
and reaches twenty-eight point
two percent on precision tasks.

00:05:19.062 --> 00:05:20.579
On a real UR5e arm:

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:23.295
five tasks, eighty trials
per task for every method,

00:05:23.335 --> 00:05:25.062
half of them under visual shifts.

00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:30.229
Gnomon4D ranks first on every task.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.315
Eighty-two point five
percent in distribution.

00:05:33.872 --> 00:05:35.900
Seventy-eight percent
under unseen lighting,

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.724
tablecloths, object
appearance, and distractors.

00:05:39.281 --> 00:05:43.133
Its lead over the strongest baseline
widens from seven points to sixteen.

00:05:45.795 --> 00:05:47.523
Ablations trace the gains.

00:05:48.064 --> 00:05:50.848
Object motion matters more
than re-encoding the arm.

00:05:51.405 --> 00:05:53.905
Guiding where and when to
read beats adding queries.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.086
And human video transfers
through GeoDelta.

00:06:00.362 --> 00:06:03.226
A sundial reads time from
the geometry of a shadow.

00:06:03.778 --> 00:06:07.380
Gnomon4D reads where and when to
act from the geometry of motion.
